            </sparkle:deltas>
        <description>&lt;h3&gt;Changed&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;**Models that reason before answering pass admission.** The default generation output budget for llama.cpp admission rises from 256 to 4,096 tokens on every path: command line, MCP tools, the app&amp;#x27;s Upgrade Engine and placement controls, and fleet commands. At 256, reasoning models such as Qwen3.6-35B-A3B and Gemma 4 12B used the whole budget thinking and were refused as truncated while answering correctly. The contract is unchanged: the exact marker, a normal stop, the throughput floors and the latency limit still apply, and a model that already stopped within 256 tokens uses the same tokens as before. Admission receipts now record the budget they ran with, and fleet benchmark and deployment calls get the same one-hour deadline as maintenance, so a reasoning model&amp;#x27;s admission on another Mac is not cut off after 30 seconds.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Corrected&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;**Managed model downloads no longer leave a copy in the temporary folder.** The Hugging Face client Spire uses (swift-huggingface 0.9.0) copied each finished download into its cache and never removed the download&amp;#x27;s temporary file, so every managed deployment left a &lt;code&gt;CFNetworkDownload_*.tmp&lt;/code&gt; clone of the model. On APFS the clone shares storage with the cached model, but it keeps that storage allocated after the model is removed. Spire now requires swift-huggingface 0.10.2, which removes it (upstream fix huggingface/swift-huggingface#65).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</description></item>
